                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(M_{\\star },\\,R_{\\star }\\) \u2013 stellar mass and radius  \n* \\(M_{p},\\,R_{p}\\) \u2013 planetary mass and radius (\\(M_{p}\\ll M_{\\star }\\))  \n* \\(a\\) \u2013 orbital semimajor axis (the separation of the two bodies)  \n* \\(i=90^{\\circ }\\) \u2013 the orbit is seen edge\u2011on, therefore the impact parameter is zero  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 orbital period (measured from the light curve)  \n* \\(\\delta\\) \u2013 fractional loss of flux during a central transit  \n* \\(\\tau\\) \u2013 total duration of the transit (first\u2013fourth contact)  \n* \\(K\\) \u2013 semi\u2011amplitude of the stellar radial\u2011velocity curve  \n\nAll quantities are assumed to be known **exactly**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Geometry of a central transit  \n\nBecause the transit goes through the centre of the stellar disc and the stellar surface brightness is uniform,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\delta=\\left(\\frac{R_{p}}{R_{\\star }}\\right)^{2}\\;}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\\boxed{k\\equiv\\frac{R_{p}}{R_{\\star }}=\\sqrt{\\delta}}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a circular orbit the planet\u2019s centre moves with the orbital speed  \n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm orb}= \\frac{2\\pi a}{P}\\quad(\\text{edge\u2011on } \\Rightarrow v_{\\rm proj}=v_{\\rm orb})\n\\]\n\nAt first contact the distance between the centres is \\(R_{\\star}+R_{p}\\); at fourth contact it is the same on the opposite side.  \nHence the chord that has to be crossed is  \n\n\\[\nL = 2\\,(R_{\\star}+R_{p}) .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the total transit duration is  \n\n\\[\n\\tau = \\frac{L}{v_{\\rm orb}}\n      =\\frac{2(R_{\\star}+R_{p})}{2\\pi a/P}\n      =\\frac{(R_{\\star}+R_{p})P}{\\pi a}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for the ratio \\(a/R_{\\star}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{a}{R_{\\star}}=\n        \\frac{1+k}{\\pi}\\,\\frac{P}{\\tau}}\n     =\\frac{1+\\sqrt{\\delta}}{\\pi}\\,\\frac{P}{\\tau}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Stellar mean density expressed only with the observables  \n\nKepler\u2019s third law (circular orbit, \\(M_{p}\\ll M_{\\star}\\))\n\n\\[\nP^{2}= \\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{G M_{\\star}}\\,a^{3}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nM_{\\star}= \\frac{4\\pi^{2}a^{3}}{G P^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nInsert the expression (2) for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\na = R_{\\star}(1+k)\\frac{P}{\\pi\\tau},\n\\qquad\na^{3}=R_{\\star}^{3}(1+k)^{3}\\frac{P^{3}}{\\pi^{3}\\tau^{3}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nM_{\\star}= \\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{G}\\,\n           \\frac{R_{\\star}^{3}(1+k)^{3}P^{3}}{\\pi^{3}\\tau^{3}P^{2}}\n         = \\frac{4R_{\\star}^{3}(1+k)^{3}P}{G\\pi\\tau^{3}} .\n\\]\n\nThe mean density is  \n\n\\[\n\\rho_{\\star}= \\frac{3M_{\\star}}{4\\pi R_{\\star}^{3}}\n            = \\frac{3(1+k)^{3}P}{G\\pi^{2}\\tau^{3}} .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(k=\\sqrt{\\delta}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\rho_{\\star}= \\frac{3\\bigl(1+\\sqrt{\\delta}\\bigr)^{3}\\,P}\n                   {G\\pi^{2}\\,\\tau^{3}} } \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich involves only the measured quantities \\(P,\\tau,\\delta\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   What the radial\u2011velocity amplitude fixes  \n\nFor a circular orbit and edge\u2011on view (\\(\\sin i =1\\))\n\n\\[\nK = \\left(\\frac{2\\pi G}{P}\\right)^{\\!1/3}\n    \\frac{M_{p}}{M_{\\star}+M_{p}}^{\\;2/3}.\n\\]\n\nWith \\(M_{p}\\ll M_{\\star}\\) we may replace \\(M_{\\star}+M_{p}\\approx M_{\\star}\\) :\n\n\\[\nK \\simeq \\left(\\frac{2\\pi G}{P}\\right)^{\\!1/3}\n          \\left(\\frac{M_{p}}{M_{\\star}}\\right)^{\\!2/3}.\n\\]\n\nHence the observable \\(K\\) determines the **mass ratio**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{M_{p}}{M_{\\star}}\n       =\\left(\\frac{K\\,P}{2\\pi G}\\right)^{\\!3/2}} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently  \n\n\\[\nM_{p}=M_{\\star}\\left(\\frac{K\\,P}{2\\pi G}\\right)^{3/2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Non\u2011uniqueness of the absolute parameters  \n\nFrom (1)\u2013(4) we see that the four observables constrain only the **ratios**\n\\(R_{p}/R_{\\star}\\), \\(a/R_{\\star}\\), \\(\\rho_{\\star}\\) and \\(M_{p}/M_{\\star}\\).\nThe absolute scales of length and mass are still free.\n\nDefine a one\u2011parameter scaling transformation with a positive factor \\(\\lambda\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{\\star}&\\longrightarrow \\lambda R_{\\star}, &\nR_{p}&\\longrightarrow \\lambda R_{p},\\\\[2pt]\na      &\\longrightarrow \\lambda a, &\nM_{\\star}&\\longrightarrow \\lambda^{-3/2}M_{\\star},\\\\[2pt]\nM_{p}  &\\longrightarrow \\lambda^{-3/2}M_{p}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n* **Depth** \\(\\delta=(R_{p}/R_{\\star})^{2}\\) is unchanged because the factor \\(\\lambda\\) cancels.\n* **Duration** \\(\\tau = (R_{\\star}+R_{p})P/(\\pi a)\\) becomes  \n  \\(\\tau'=\\lambda(R_{\\star}+R_{p})P/[\\pi(\\lambda a)]=\\tau\\).\n* **Period** follows from Kepler\u2019s law:\n  \\[\n  P'^{2}= \\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{G M_{\\star}'}\\,(a')^{3}\n        =\\frac{4\\pi^{2}}{G\\,\\lambda^{-3/2}M_{\\star}}\\,\n          (\\lambda a)^{3}=P^{2},\n  \\]\n  so \\(P'=P\\).\n* **Radial\u2011velocity amplitude** depends only on the mass ratio and on \\(P\\):\n  \\[\n  K'=\\left(\\frac{2\\pi G}{P'}\\right)^{1/3}\n      \\Bigl(\\frac{M_{p}'}{M_{\\star}'}\\Bigr)^{2/3}\n     =\\left(\\frac{2\\pi G}{P}\\right)^{1/3}\n      \\Bigl(\\frac{\\lambda^{-3/2}M_{p}}{\\lambda^{-3/2}M_{\\star}}\\Bigr)^{2/3}=K .\n  \\]\n\nThus the transformation (5) leaves **all four observables invariant**, proving that the absolute values of \\(M_{\\star},R_{\\star},M_{p},R_{p}\\) cannot be recovered uniquely from them alone.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Determining the absolute parameters once \\(R_{\\star}\\) is known  \n\nAssume an independent measurement supplies the true stellar radius \\(R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}}\\).\n\n1. **Stellar mass** \u2013 use the density obtained in (3):\n   \\[\n   \\rho_{\\star}= \\frac{3\\bigl(1+\\sqrt{\\delta}\\bigr)^{3}P}{G\\pi^{2}\\tau^{3}},\n   \\qquad\n   M_{\\star}= \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\,\\rho_{\\star}\\,(R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}})^{3}\n            = \\frac{4\\bigl(1+\\sqrt{\\delta}\\bigr)^{3}P}{G\\pi\\tau^{3}}\\,\n              (R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}})^{3}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Planetary radius** \u2013 directly from the depth:\n   \\[\n   R_{p}=k\\,R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}}\n        =\\sqrt{\\delta}\\;R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Planetary mass** \u2013 combine (4) with the now\u2011known stellar mass:\n   \\[\n   M_{p}=M_{\\star}\\left(\\frac{K\\,P}{2\\pi G}\\right)^{3/2}\n        =\\frac{4\\bigl(1+\\sqrt{\\delta}\\bigr)^{3}P}{G\\pi\\tau^{3}}\\,\n          (R_{\\star}^{\\;{\\rm abs}})^{3}\n          \\left(\\frac{K\\,P}{2\\pi G}\\right)^{3/2}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\nEquations (6)\u2013(8) give the unique physical parameters consistent with the four measured observables together with the externally supplied stellar radius.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary**\n\n| Quantity | Expression in terms of the observables |\n|----------|----------------------------------------|\n| \\(k=R_{p}/R_{\\star}\\) | \\(\\displaystyle k=\\sqrt{\\delta}\\) |\n| \\(a/R_{\\star}\\) | \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{R_{\\star}}=\\frac{1+\\sqrt{\\delta}}{\\pi}\\frac{P}{\\tau}\\) |\n| \\(\\rho_{\\star}\\) | \\(\\displaystyle \\rho_{\\star}= \\frac{3\\bigl(1+\\sqrt{\\delta}\\bigr)^{3}P}{G\\pi^{2}\\tau^{3}}\\) |\n| Mass ratio | \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{M_{p}}{M_{\\star}}=\\left(\\frac{K\\,P}{2\\pi G}\\right)^{3/2}\\) |\n| Scaling symmetry | \\((R_{\\star},R_{p},a)\\to\\lambda(\\cdot),\\;(M_{\\star},M_{p})\\to\\lambda^{-3/2}(\\cdot)\\) leaves \\(P,\\delta,\\tau,K\\) unchanged |\n| Absolute parameters (if \\(R_{\\star}\\) known) | Eq.\u202f(6)\u2013(8) above |\n\nThese results answer parts (1)\u2013(5) of the problem.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer each part with derivations.\n\nGiven transit depth \u03b4 = (Rp/R*)\u00b2 because central transit, uniform brightness. So k = sqrt(\u03b4).\n\nTransit geometry: For circular orbit, impact parameter b=0 (central). Total duration \u03c4 = (2 R* + 2 a sin i)/v_orb? Actually time from first to fourth contact equals time to cross chord length across star plus extra due to planet size? For central transit, chord length = 2 R*. But also planet blocks some portion; but total duration includes time for planet to move from first to fourth contact, which corresponds to moving across diameter of star plus its own radius? Let's recall formula: For central transit, total duration \u03c4 = (2 R* + 2 Rp)/v where v = orbital speed projected onto line of sight = (2\u03c0 a)/P (since edge-on). However more precisely, the chord length across star is 2 R*, but the planet's leading edge enters at first contact when distance between centers = R* + Rp. At that moment, the planet's limb touches star limb. Similarly exit when distance = R* + Rp on opposite side. So the path length along line of sight between contacts is 2 (R* + Rp). Therefore \u03c4 = 2 (R* + Rp) / v_proj. Since edge-on, v_proj = v_orb = 2\u03c0 a / P. Thus:\n\n\u03c4 = 2 (R* + Rp) * P / (2\u03c0 a) = (R* + Rp) P / (\u03c0 a).\n\nThus a/R* = (R* + Rp) P / (\u03c0 \u03c4) * (1/R*)? Wait solve for a/R*: Multiply both sides: \u03c4 = (R* + Rp) P / (\u03c0 a). Rearranged: a = (R* + Rp) P / (\u03c0 \u03c4). Divide by R*: a/R* = (1 + Rp/R*) P / (\u03c0 \u03c4) = (1 + k) P / (\u03c0 \u03c4).\n\nSo expression uses \u03b4 via k = sqrt(\u03b4). Good.\n\nNow (2): Use Kepler's third law: P\u00b2 = (4\u03c0\u00b2/G M_total) a\u00b3. With M_p << M_*, M_total \u2248 M_*. So M_* = (4\u03c0\u00b2 a\u00b3)/(G P\u00b2). Density \u03c1_* = 3 M_*/(4\u03c0 R_*^3). Substitute a/R* expression.\n\nCompute a = (R* + Rp) P/(\u03c0 \u03c4). Write a = R* (1+k) P/(\u03c0 \u03c4). Then a\u00b3 = R*\u00b3 (1+k)\u00b3 P\u00b3 /(\u03c0\u00b3 \u03c4\u00b3). Plug into M_*:\n\nM_* = (4\u03c0\u00b2 / G) * a\u00b3 / P\u00b2 = (4\u03c0\u00b2 / G) * [R*\u00b3 (1+k)\u00b3 P\u00b3 /(\u03c0\u00b3 \u03c4\u00b3)] / P\u00b2 = (4\u03c0\u00b2 / G) * R*\u00b3 (1+k)\u00b3 P / (\u03c0\u00b3 \u03c4\u00b3) = (4 R*\u00b3 (1+k)\u00b3 P) / (G \u03c0 \u03c4\u00b3).\n\nThen density:\n\n\u03c1_* = 3 M_*/(4\u03c0 R*\u00b3) = 3/(4\u03c0 R*\u00b3) * (4 R*\u00b3 (1+k)\u00b3 P)/(G \u03c0 \u03c4\u00b3) = (3 (1+k)\u00b3 P)/(G \u03c0\u00b2 \u03c4\u00b3).\n\nSimplify: \u03c1_* = (3 (1+\u221a\u03b4)\u00b3 P)/(G \u03c0\u00b2 \u03c4\u00b3). That's answer.\n\n(3): Radial velocity semi-amplitude K = (2\u03c0 G / P)^{1/3} * (M_p sin i) / (M_* + M_p)^{2/3} * (1/\u221a(1 - e\u00b2)) etc. For circular orbit, i=90\u00b0, sin i =1. Approximate M_p << M_*, then K \u2248 (2\u03c0 G / P)^{1/3} * (M_p / M_*)^{2/3}. More precisely K = (2\u03c0 G / P)^{1/3} * (M_p / (M_* + M_p))^{2/3} * (1/\u221a(1-e\u00b2)). With e=0. So K \u221d (M_p / M_*)^{2/3}. Hence ratio M_p / M_* = (K P / (2\u03c0 G))^{3/2}. So combination fixed: M_p / M_* = (K P / (2\u03c0 G))^{3/2}. Or equivalently M_p = M_* (K P / (2\u03c0 G))^{3/2}.\n\nBut we don't know M_* yet; this gives relation.\n\n(4): Show non-uniqueness: scaling transformation: multiply all lengths (R*, a, Rp) by factor \u03bb, keep masses scaled appropriately? Need to keep observables unchanged. Observables: P depends on a and masses via Kepler: P \u221d a^{3/2} (M_*+M_p)^{-1/2}. If we scale a \u2192 \u03bb a, and simultaneously scale M_* \u2192 \u03bb^{-3/2} M_*? Let's test invariance.\n\nGoal: find transformation such that P, \u03b4, \u03c4, K remain same.\n\n\u03b4 = (Rp/R*)^2 independent of scaling if both radii scale equally. So require \u03bb cancels: (\u03bb Rp / \u03bb R*)^2 = (Rp/R*)^2. OK.\n\n\u03c4 = (R* + Rp) P / (\u03c0 a). Under scaling: new \u03c4' = (\u03bb(R*+Rp)) P' / (\u03c0 \u03bb a) = (R*+Rp) P' / (\u03c0 a). To keep \u03c4 unchanged, need P' = P. So scaling must leave P unchanged.\n\nK depends on masses and a: K \u221d (M_p / M_*)^{2/3} (a/P)^{1/3}? Actually K = (2\u03c0 G / P)^{1/3} (M_p / (M_*+M_p))^{2/3}. No explicit a. So scaling masses while keeping ratio constant will keep K unchanged. So choose scaling that keeps M_p/M_* ratio constant.\n\nThus we can define a one-parameter family: pick any \u03bb>0, set R* \u2192 \u03bb R*, Rp \u2192 \u03bb Rp (so \u03b4 unchanged), a \u2192 \u03bb a (to keep \u03c4 unchanged given P unchanged). Keep masses unchanged? But then P would change because P \u221d a^{3/2}/M_*^{1/2}. If we increase a without changing M_*, P changes, breaking invariance. Instead we adjust M_* accordingly: let M_* \u2192 \u03bb^{-3/2} M_* . Then a scales \u03bb, so a^{3/2} \u221d \u03bb^{3/2}, M_*^{1/2} \u221d \u03bb^{-3/4}, product yields P unchanged. Also need to keep M_p/M_* ratio constant, so M_p \u2192 \u03bb^{-3/2} M_* * (M_p/M_*) = \u03bb^{-3/2} M_p. That maintains ratio. Then K remains same because ratio unchanged and P unchanged. So transformation: (R*, a, Rp) \u2192 \u03bb times themselves; (M_*, M_p) \u2192 \u03bb^{-3/2} times themselves. This leaves all observables invariant. Provide proof.\n\n(5): Given absolute R* known, we can compute M_* from density formula derived earlier: \u03c1_* = 3 M_*/(4\u03c0 R*\u00b3). So M_* = (4\u03c0/3) \u03c1_* R*\u00b3. Use expression for \u03c1_* from (2): \u03c1_* = (3 (1+\u221a\u03b4)\u00b3 P)/(G \u03c0\u00b2 \u03c4\u00b3). So M_* = (4\u03c0/3) * (3 (1+\u221a\u03b4)\u00b3 P)/(G \u03c0\u00b2 \u03c4\u00b3) * R*\u00b3 = (4 (1+\u221a\u03b4)\u00b3 P R*\u00b3)/(G \u03c0 \u03c4\u00b3).\n\nThen Rp = k R* = \u221a\u03b4 R*.\n\nFinally M_p from relation in (3): M_p = M_* (K P / (2\u03c0 G))^{3/2}. Insert M_* expression.\n\nThus provide formulas.\n\nLet's craft final answer with clear steps."
